INT. LIBRARY INSIDE GIANT TORTOISE SHELL - NIGHT

A cathedral of bone-white arches curving overhead, every rib lined with shelves of crumbling scrolls. Lanterns hang on slow chains. The floor sways in a gentle, immense rhythm, as though the whole library were breathing.

ARCHIVIST ELOWEN, thirties, in a gray robe, ink on her fingers, climbs a rolling ladder with a scroll tucked under her arm.

A STRANGER, forties, in a travel-worn cloak, steps through the narrow doorway at the base of the shell. Snow dusts his shoulders. He sets down a staff carved with spirals.

STRANGER
Forgive the hour. I seek the book of true names.

ELOWEN
No one seeks it. It finds them.

STRANGER
Then perhaps it has found me.

ELOWEN
Perhaps. Your name?

STRANGER
I have not carried one in a long time.

She descends. The ladder creaks. She walks to him and studies his face by lantern light, the scar through his left brow, the grey in his beard.

ELOWEN
I know that scar.

STRANGER
I doubt it.

ELOWEN
You were a prince, once. Of the drowned towns.

STRANGER
I was a boy once. A prince is only a boy with a good tailor.

She reaches for a high shelf and brings down a bound volume, its cover cracked, its clasp rusted. She opens it, turns three pages, and reads aloud.

ELOWEN
"Tamsin, son of the tide. Sworn to forget, and failing."

The Stranger closes his eyes. The floor lurches, a great slow tremor, as the tortoise stirs beneath them. Dust falls from the arches.

TAMSIN
You have spoken it.

ELOWEN
Tamsin.

TAMSIN
Then you know what comes.

ELOWEN
The sea.

He nods. Far below the floor, something vast begins to move toward the sound of his name.
